WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.250
On a tray lies a miniature Japan made of oatmeal: every flake a city.

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:15.140
A yellow, single-celled smear placed at Tokyo connects them within hours, and the network it draws is nearly as efficient as the rail system engineers spent decades building.

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.230
This thing has no brain, no nerves — it is not even more than one cell.

4
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:34.640
In a laboratory at Hokkaido University, a thin layer of agar had been poured across a flat tray.

5
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:43.890
Agar is a jelly-like growth medium used in labs; it is made from seaweed, it is transparent, and it gives whatever is placed on it both a floor and moisture.

6
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.220
This tray was no ordinary petri dish.

7
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:54.070
The edges of the jelly had been cut with great care, and the shape they were cut into was a map of Japan's Kanto plain.

8
00:00:54.070 --> 00:01:02.840
The notch of Tokyo Bay, the line where the sea meets the shore, the places where the mountains rise; all of it was there, to scale.

9
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:10.970
The sea and the mountains were left without medium, or else lit with a strong light, because the star of this experiment flees from bright light.

10
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:16.860
In other words, the researchers had written geography itself into the dish as an obstacle.

11
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.230
Then came the oat flakes.

12
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:25.920
36 small pieces of oat were placed one by one, each at the true position of a city around Tokyo.

13
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:33.250
One where Yokohama is, one where Chiba is, one for Utsunomiya in the north, one for Hachioji in the west.

14
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.340
The map was ready, the cities were in place, and between them there was nothing at all.

15
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:46.950
At the point where Tokyo lies, however, what was placed was not an oat flake but a yellowish, veined, moist piece of tissue.

16
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:52.360
The name of that piece of tissue is Physarum polycephalum, the many-headed slime mould.

17
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:58.570
It has mould in its name, but it is not a fungus; it is not a plant either, nor an animal.

18
00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.340
It belongs to a separate branch known as the amoebozoans.

19
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:14.350
And here is the most astonishing thing about it: that yellow patch, the size of a hand, sometimes the size of a plate, exceeding 30 centimetres under ideal conditions, is a single cell.

20
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.320
One undivided cell, not partitioned by any walls.

21
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.250
Millions of nuclei float inside it, but all of them lie beneath the same membrane.

22
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.620
It has no nerve cells.

23
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.990
It has no brain.

24
00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.960
It has no eyes, no ears, no centre.

25
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:39.450
It lives under rotting logs in forests, eats bacteria and fungal spores, and travels a few centimetres an hour.

26
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.420
There was a history behind seating this creature at such a table.

27
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:51.870
In 2000, Toshiyuki Nakagaki, who headed the same group, had published a half-page note in the British science journal Nature.

28
00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.640
The claim in its title was blunt: an amoeba was solving a maze.

29
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.010
The experiment was simple.

30
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:06.820
The slime mould had been spread through a small maze partitioned by plastic walls, with an oat flake placed at the entrance and another at the exit.

31
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:14.150
There were four possible routes from entrance to exit; the shortest was about 33 millimetres, the longest 45.

32
00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:19.080
At the start, the creature was everywhere in the maze, filling every corridor.

33
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.210
After roughly four hours it had withdrawn from the dead ends.

34
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:29.900
Four hours later, only one thing remained: a single thick tube joining the two oat flakes by the shortest route.

35
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.190
The other three routes had been abandoned, absorbed, erased.

36
00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:42.160
A cell without a brain had solved a problem that humans solve by drawing on paper, and it had solved it by reshaping its own body.

37
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.970
The Tokyo tray was that idea, scaled up.

38
00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:52.660
In the maze there had been two points; here there were 36, and it was not obvious what the right answer even was.

39
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.110
The camera was set to time-lapse, and the waiting began.

40
00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:01.080
In the first hours the patch spread with an appetite that would not stop.

41
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:09.370
It widened outward from Tokyo like a wave, engulfed every oat flake in its path, and covered almost the whole plain in a yellow sheet.

42
00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.340
At this stage there was no network; there was an occupation.

43
00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.870
Then the second phase began.

44
00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:21.760
The creature started to abandon most of the area it had taken, to pull its own mass back in.

45
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.770
The blindness of spreading gave way to a choosing.

46
00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.220
Some arms thickened from one day to the next; others thinned and snapped.

47
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.790
The sheet grew sparse, and as it thinned, a skeleton emerged beneath it.

48
00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:40.520
At the end of 26 hours, the image on the tray threw everyone who looked at it to the same place.

49
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:47.050
The yellow tubes linking the oat flakes overlapped, uncannily, with the lines of Tokyo's rail network.

50
00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:54.700
Thick trunk corridors opening outward from the centre, ring links joining the outer cities to one another, curves bending around the mountain.

51
00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:07.470
The rough plan of a network that was the product of decades of engineers, planners, land surveys, budget arguments and political bargaining had been drawn by a single cell in a day and two hours, while chasing oats.

52
00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:14.320
The findings were published in the 22 January 2010 issue of the American science journal Science.

53
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:24.530
The paper's first author was Atsushi Tero, then working at Hokkaido; Nakagaki, the mathematician Ryo Kobayashi and Mark Fricker of the University of Oxford were among the others.

54
00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:34.420
That same year the team received the Ig Nobel Prize, given for research that first makes you laugh and then makes you think, in the category of transportation planning.

55
00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:40.470
For Nakagaki it was the second; the maze experiment had won the same prize in 2008.

56
00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:45.080
And once the smile of the award had faded, a single question remained.

57
00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.290
How does this thing happen?

58
00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:51.580
The search for an answer has to begin by accepting what the creature is not.

59
00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.110
There is no decision centre.

60
00:05:54.110 --> 00:06:04.480
There is no manager cell sitting somewhere on the tray, looking at the map and saying, "let's cancel that line." Nor is there a nervous system gathering and processing signals.

61
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:10.850
All there is is a plumbing system of fluid-filled tubes, and the cytoplasm flowing inside that plumbing.

62
00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:15.140
The way this plumbing works is the slime mould's most distinctive feature.

63
00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:21.350
In the walls of the tubes there are proteins much like those in our muscle cells: actin and myosin.

64
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:29.960
Those walls contract and relax rhythmically; a wave of contraction travels along the tubes, raising the pressure in one place and lowering it in another.

65
00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.730
The result is a flow that runs back and forth like the shuttle on a weaving loom.

66
00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:43.020
The fluid moves in one direction at speeds reaching a millimetre per second, then, after about one to two minutes, reverses and comes back.

67
00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:46.990
The entire body stirs its own interior without pause.

68
00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:53.040
Nutrients, signalling molecules and chemical messages circulate on this coming and going current.

69
00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.170
And this is exactly where the network's self-shaping comes from.

70
00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:06.900
The rule is simple enough to write in a single line: a tube through which plenty of fluid passes grows thicker, and a tube through which little passes grows thinner and is eventually absorbed.

71
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.990
To see how powerful that rule is, it is enough to recall how fluids behave.

72
00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:21.240
The resistance met by fluid passing through a tube is extremely sensitive to the tube's width; widening the tube a little lowers the resistance by a far greater proportion.

73
00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:26.650
Which means the thick tube pulls flow towards itself, at the expense of the thin one.

74
00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:33.180
As flow increases the wall relaxes more, the tube widens further, and it draws still more flow.

75
00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.550
This is a snowball effect.

76
00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:42.560
There is a pressure in the opposite direction too: every unused tube steadily weakens, thins and breaks.

77
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.650
The creature takes the material it has committed to an unused line and invests it in a used one.

78
00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.460
Now think about the maze with that in mind.

79
00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:59.910
At the start the creature fills every corridor, there are four separate lines between the two oat flakes, and all four connect the same two points.

80
00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:04.680
But the short line carries the same quantity of fluid with less resistance.

81
00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.330
The flow through the short line is a little greater.

82
00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:17.420
A little more flow means a slightly thicker tube; the thickening tube draws still more flow on the next pass, and the long lines quietly dry out.

83
00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:25.230
In the dead ends there is no flow at all; since no fluid can enter at one end and leave at the same end, those stretches never even enter the race.

84
00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.760
Nobody calculates anything.

85
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.890
Physics alone, by itself, accumulates the right answer.

86
00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:36.980
This is why the slime mould's memory bears no resemblance to what we call memory.

87
00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:43.670
The route it has learned is not a record stored somewhere; it is the very geometry of its body.

88
00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:48.760
A thick tube is the sentence "this way is worth travelling," made flesh.

89
00:08:48.760 --> 00:09:00.570
A study published in 2021 in the journal of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States pushed the idea one step further: Mirna Kramar and Karen Alim showed that when the creature touches a food source,

90
00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:10.300
a softening substance is released into the tubes from that point, that this substance spreads through the network with the flow, and that it widens the tubes it passes through.

91
00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:16.190
Which means the information about where food is found gets engraved into the hierarchy of tube thicknesses.

92
00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:18.880
The body is its own map.

93
00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:23.970
Nakagaki and Kobayashi turned this logic into equations.

94
00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:34.340
In the model each tube carries a conductivity value, flow is distributed according to that conductivity, and conductivity in turn grows or shrinks according to the flow it carries.

95
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:45.510
The result was both surprising and reassuring: without watching a real creature on a tray, simply by turning these two rules through numbers, they could produce networks resembling the one on the Tokyo tray.

96
00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:56.840
What is more, adjusting a single coefficient in the equation changed the character of the network; sometimes out came the cheapest, barest possible skeleton, sometimes a weave with spare routes,

97
00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.010
more expensive but more resistant to breaks.

98
00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.540
Then the mathematicians took over.

99
00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:12.590
In 2012, Vincenzo Bonifaci, Kurt Mehlhorn and Girish Varma proved that this system of equations really does converge on the shortest path between two points,

100
00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:18.640
independently of the shape of the network and of the initial distribution of mass.

101
00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:23.730
So the slime mould's solution is not a coincidence or a piece of laboratory luck.

102
00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.580
It is hidden in the rule itself.

103
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.110
But is a thickening tube thinking?

104
00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:36.600
There is a debate around that question that continues to this day and sharpens from time to time, and both sides have serious grounds.

105
00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:45.050
The sceptics' objection is direct: what we are seeing here, they say, is not intelligence but a minimisation process.

106
00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:48.700
Nature is full of such things.

107
00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:59.710
Dip a wire frame into soapy water and pull it out, and the film builds the surface that joins several points with the least possible surface area; nobody calculated it, surface tension did.

108
00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:03.840
Rivers on their way to the sea find the bed of least resistance.

109
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.130
Lightning picks the corridor in the air that ionises most easily.

110
00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:17.540
The thickening of the slime mould's tubes in proportion to flow belongs to the same family: a flow network reorganises itself so as to reduce its loss of energy.

111
00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:25.190
On this view, saying "the blob designed the Tokyo metro" is as figurative as saying "the soap film solved a geometry problem."

112
00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:29.640
The other side says that this analogy jams up beyond a certain point.

113
00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:36.010
A soap film does not go hungry, does not keep its past experience, does not acquire habits.

114
00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:39.020
The slime mould does.

115
00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:51.150
In 2016, Romain Boisseau, David Vogel and Audrey Dussutour, working in Toulouse, built a gel bridge that the creature had to cross in order to reach an oat flake, and laced the bridge with bitter substances,

116
00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:54.000
quinine or caffeine.

117
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.770
In the first days the creature entered the bridge reluctantly, slowly.

118
00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:07.540
As the days passed the hesitation diminished; by the end of the sixth day it was advancing as fast as the control group crossing a harmless bridge.

119
00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:12.470
Then, when the substance was removed for a while, the wariness came back.

120
00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:19.800
This is a demonstration of habituation in an organism with no nervous system; one of the most basic forms of learning.

121
00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:30.010
The same team also points back to older findings showing that the creature can anticipate a periodically repeated drying or cooling event in its environment and begin to slow down before it arrives.

122
00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:38.300
Even so, to stay honest about the real subject at hand, a few things that have been exaggerated about the Tokyo experiment need correcting.

123
00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:43.870
First, the slime mould did not design the railway and did not "beat" the engineers.

124
00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:55.200
The comparison the researchers made was far more measured: the network the creature built was comparable to the real rail network in total tube length, that is cost, in average distance between cities,

125
00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:01.090
that is efficiency, and in tolerance to the failure of a random link, that is robustness.

126
00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:06.340
On some measures it was a shade ahead, and on resistance to breaks somewhat behind.

127
00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:12.230
What there was, was not a superiority but an equivalence, and that was precisely the striking part.

128
00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:15.720
Second, the tray was not Tokyo.

129
00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:27.850
Most of the constraints that draw a real railway network were not present on that jelly: land ownership, the cost of expropriation, the geology in which a tunnel can be dug, the distribution of population over time,

130
00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:31.660
which city carried political weight in which period.

131
00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:38.510
The slime mould did not know the cities' populations either; every oat flake was a reward of equal size to it.

132
00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:46.160
The answer to the experiment was already known, and the obstacles were built around the geography that had produced that answer.

133
00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:54.450
This does not make the result worthless; but it does leave the sentence "a brainless creature planned better than Japanese engineers" without support.

134
00:13:54.450 --> 00:14:00.820
Third, and most importantly: no mystical conclusion follows from any of this.

135
00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:08.950
There is no question of the slime mould knowing Tokyo, recognising cities, pursuing a purpose or carrying some enigmatic consciousness.

136
00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:17.240
What is there is a well-understood mechanism; chemical signals, rhythms of contraction, flow, and feedback accumulating in tubes.

137
00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:23.770
Claims that go beyond that are products of imagination, not of the present scientific consensus.

138
00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:26.300
So what is left?

139
00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:32.670
I think the real legacy of the experiment is not the answer itself but where the answer came from.

140
00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:43.840
There is a family of algorithms in the engineering literature today inspired by the slime mould; they are used on problems like network design, route planning and the placement of communication lines.

141
00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:48.610
But the appeal of those algorithms comes less from their speed than from their architecture.

142
00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:55.620
Because our way of building networks is almost always central: a desk, a map, a planner, an approval.

143
00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.990
The slime mould's is not.

144
00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.800
No point in its network has information about the whole.

145
00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:07.530
Each tube only "knows" how much passes through itself, and thickens or thins accordingly.

146
00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:13.740
Out of a local, stupid, one-line rule repeated millions of times, a solution that sees the whole is born.

147
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:21.710
What this tells us is as illuminating as it is unsettling: for a system to become optimised, it does not need anyone optimising it.

148
00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:25.360
The same logic meets us elsewhere in the world.

149
00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:31.730
Our blood vessels also mature by pruning the little-used branches and thickening the ones through which much flows.

150
00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.740
The veins of a leaf obey the same economy.

151
00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:39.190
The synapses in our brains also strengthen and weaken according to use.

152
00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:49.560
The slime mould is the barest, most visible member of this family; because, having no skull to hide it in, it does all its reckoning out in the open, on the tray, as a yellow line.

153
00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:54.490
And perhaps that was the genuinely disquieting thing about that time-lapse footage.

154
00:15:54.490 --> 00:16:03.900
Watching 26 hours compressed into a minute on the screen, nobody looking at that yellow patch spreading, then withdrawing, then appearing to have made up its mind,

155
00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:08.190
thought that what they were seeing was merely a flowing fluid.

156
00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:17.440
The question we are forced to sit with is this: if we look at what it does and say "that is just physics," why do we not say the same about what goes on inside our own heads?

157
00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.410
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

158
00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:24.740
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
